"All done — the plan was executed end to end and verification passed. Summary:\n\n**Files created** (in `/home/agent/workspace`):\n- `messy_data.csv` — 12 rows of intentionally dirty data\n- `clean_data.py` — stdlib-only cleaner (csv + datetime + statistics)\n- `cleaned_data.csv` — the verified output\n\n**What the cleaner fixed:**\n| Problem | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Exact duplicate row (John Smith ×2) | Dropped → 11 rows |\n| Missing/invalid ages (`,`, `abc`, `230`) | Filled with median (36) — 3 fills |\n| Mixed date formats (ISO, DD/MM/YYYY, `2024.04.01`, \"March 3, 2024\"…) | All normalized to ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` |\n| Stray whitespace & inconsistent casing | Trimmed, names/cities title-cased, emails lowercased |\n| Malformed email (`not-an-email`) | Blank, flagged in the summary |\n\n**Verification asserts (all green):** 11 rows · every date ISO-formatted · every age an integer in 0–120 · no stray whitespace · no duplicate emails.\n\nOne thing worth knowing: I caught and fixed a mid-run issue where median-filled ages printed as `36.0` (float); the final output writes clean integers. Also note `carol king`'s date `2024.04.01` parses as April 1 — if your data ever mixes `DD.MM.YYYY` dot formats, that's an ambiguity to handle explicitly."